---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Three generations of the same pipeline — Andrew Yang"
  description="A file-based queue, then a real broker with retry topology, then a five-service pipeline with an editorial gate — each one forced by the last one's wall."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">async pipelines</span>
  <h1>Three generations of the same pipeline</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The problem never changed: take content in, enrich it with AI, publish it
    somewhere durable. I have now built that pipeline three times. Each
    generation exists because the previous one hit a wall worth naming.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 140"
    alt="Three generations left to right: a file-based queue, then a RabbitMQ pipeline with delivery guarantees, then a five-service pipeline with an editorial gate. Each arrow is labelled with the wall that forced the next generation: files have no failure semantics; a reliable pipeline still has no judgment."
    caption="The arrows are the story: each generation was forced by a named failure of the last, and each kept the invariant — async, idempotent, inspectable."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 1 — files as a queue" sub="YAML queue, two-phase AI workflow" sub2="idempotent, operator-supervised" />
    <DBox x={335} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 2 — a real broker" sub="retry ladder, DLQ replay," sub2="cost circuit breaker" />
    <DBox x={660} y={36} w={230} h={64} accent title="Gen 3 — with an opinion" sub="five services + editorial gate" sub2="backed by the reading corpus" />
    <path class="flow" d="M240,68 L329,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="26" text-anchor="middle">no failure</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="120" text-anchor="middle">semantics</text>
    <path class="flow" d="M565,68 L654,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="26" text-anchor="middle">reliable, but</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="120" text-anchor="middle">no judgment</text>
  </Diagram>

  <h2>Generation 1 — a queue made of files</h2>
  <p>
    The book-repository provisioning CLI: a YAML queue on disk, a two-phase
    workflow where the CLI writes a request file, an AI session answers into a
    response file, and the CLI resumes. Idempotent by design — re-runs are safe,
    duplicates are detected against a cached index.
  </p>
  <p>
    It works, and still runs today. Its wall: <strong>files are a queue with no
    semantics</strong>. No retry, no dead-lettering, no delivery guarantee, no
    observability. Fine when the operator is the failure handler; a dead end the
    moment anything must run unattended.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 2 — a real broker, learned properly</h2>
  <p>
    A rebuild on RabbitMQ — ai-radar, the system later renamed bookshelf-echo —
    gave the idea the semantics gen 1 lacked: idempotent consumers, a TTL+DLX
    retry ladder, DLQ replay tooling, and an LLM cost circuit breaker. This is
    where I learned what a delivery guarantee actually costs to provide.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its wall was different: <strong>a pipeline that only moves data is not yet a
    product</strong>. Radar-style output (here is everything that happened) has
    no editorial judgment — and judgment was the interesting part.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 3 — a pipeline with an opinion</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`${base}/demo/bookshelf-echo/`}>bookshelf-echo</a>, the production
    successor: five services (producers →
    enricher → matcher → digester → publisher) around the same broker patterns,
    plus the two things gen 2 was missing.
  </p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>An editorial gate:</strong> every news item is matched against my
      library of ~{facts.booksIndexed} books through a vector-search API (the
      <a href={`${base}/lab/knowledge-as-infrastructure/`}>knowledge engine</a>).
      Only items that <em>resonate with something I have actually read</em> pass —
      the pipeline publishes echoes, not headlines.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A security posture as an ADR:</strong> zero-inbound — every port
      binds loopback, with each exception (metrics scrape, management UI behind
      the edge) documented as a deliberate breach of the rule, not an accident.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A durable output end:</strong> a publish queue feeding a static
      site generator, so the pipeline's product is a website, not a log.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What forced each evolution</h2>
  <p>
    Gen 1 → 2: the need to run unattended exposed that files have no failure
    semantics. Gen 2 → 3: running reliably exposed that reliability was never
    the point — <em>selection</em> was. Each generation kept the invariant
    (async, idempotent, inspectable) and replaced exactly the layer that failed.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Don't skip generation 1. The file queue taught the shape of the problem for
    the price of a weekend; the broker then had a real workload to justify every
    piece of its complexity. Architecture bought before the failure that demands
    it is just inventory.
  </p>
</Base>
